#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222397} :activity/id #uuid "2774bc0e-42a3-5956-a4b7-a719151ea7c2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049288 :topic/id #uuid "ea0735e9-7712-5a1c-a626-25cab33f54cb" :topic/math-academy-id 6386} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222397 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222522 :tutorial/content "Suppose a linear ODE has a periodic forcing term whose Fourier series is known, for example\n\n$$\ny' + ky = f(x),\n\\qquad\\text{where}\\qquad\nf(x) \\sim \\frac{c_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\Big(c_n\\cos(\\omega_n x) + d_n\\sin(\\omega_n x)\\Big).\n$$\n\nA standard strategy is to look for a *particular* solution with the *same Fourier frequencies*:\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\frac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\Big(a_n\\cos(\\omega_n x) + b_n\\sin(\\omega_n x)\\Big)\n$$\n\nThen, the method includes the following steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Differentiate term-by-term.\n\nUsing $\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\cos(\\omega_n x)=-\\omega_n\\sin(\\omega_n x)$ and $\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\sin(\\omega_n x)=\\omega_n\\cos(\\omega_n x)$, we get\n\n$$\ny_p' \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\Big(-a_n\\omega_n\\sin(\\omega_n x) + b_n\\omega_n\\cos(\\omega_n x)\\Big).\n$$\n\n- **Step 2:** Substitute into the ODE and collect like terms\n\nThen, we plug $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the equation, and rewrite the left-hand side as\n\n$$\n\\Big(\\substack{\\large\\text{free} \\[3pt] \\large\\text{term}}\\Big) \\;+\\; \n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\Big(\\substack{\\large\\text{cosine} \\[3pt] \\large\\text{coefficient}}\\Big) \\cos(\\omega_n x) \\;+\\; \n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\Big(\\substack{\\large\\text{sine} \\[3pt] \\large\\text{coefficient}}\\Big) \\sin(\\omega_n x).\n$$\n\n- **Step 3:** Equate coefficients.\n\nBecause the Fourier series representation is unique (up to convergence issues we ignore here), we match:\n\n- the free terms, and\n\n- for each $n\\ge 1$, the cosine coefficients and sine coefficients.\n\nThis produces:\n\n- one equation for $a_0$ (from the constant term), and\n\n- a $2\\times 2$ linear system for $(a_n,b_n)$ for each $n\\ge 1$.\n\n- **Step 4:** Write the Fourier series of $y_p.$\n\nAfter solving for $a_0$, $a_n$, and $b_n$, substitute them back into\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\frac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\Big(a_n\\cos(\\omega_n x) + b_n\\sin(\\omega_n x)\\Big).\n$$\n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "84328521-7170-5404-8ec2-a41475c2ad15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14711 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0521dca3-2fe6-50aa-8dc9-282e35ffe718" :step/math-academy-id 607546 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222398}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222398 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221455 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221203 :question/id #uuid "03fbfe2c-b12f-5220-9fae-e0dfe210a505" :question/math-academy-id "e-21857"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4733fab7-18ca-5336-9aba-53212ddbf87b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050010}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397676}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving First Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"} :step/id #uuid "ab947003-2e2f-5620-996d-e8070699c2a1" :step/math-academy-id 607547 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222399}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222399 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222523 :tutorial/content "For second-order linear ODEs, the overall algorithm is the same as for first-order equations: \n\n- we assume a Fourier series for $y_p$,\n\n- substitute into the ODE, and\n\n- match coefficients.\n\nBefore we start, we can use the forcing series properties to simplify our work. For a second-order equation of the form \n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = f(x)\n$$\n\nwith no $y'$ (damping) term, the particular solution can be simplified.\n\nThe key insight comes from the fact that the differential operator \n\n$$\nL[y] = y'' + \\omega^2 y\n$$\n\nmaps cosines to cosines and sines to sines. This means we can match the form of our assumed solution $y_p$ to the form of the forcing function $f(x),$ which saves a great deal of work.\n\nThis leads to some powerful shortcuts:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is a *pure cosine series* (an even function), we can assume $y_p$ is also a pure cosine series (all $b_n=0$).\n\n- If $f(x)$ is a *pure sine series* (an odd function), we can assume $y_p$ is also a pure sine series (all $a_n=0$, including $a_0=0$).\n\n- If the constant term in the series for $f(x)$ is zero, the constant term in $y_p$ will also be zero (so $a_0=0$).\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "12f51f58-a298-5fc1-9540-6d9f4611481b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14712 :tutorial/title "Second Order ODEs With Fourier Series"} :step/id #uuid "d1b21edf-14d7-5094-a313-f622ad12149b" :step/math-academy-id 607550 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222403}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222400 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221456 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221204 :question/id #uuid "3944f6e4-a3a8-5bad-87c5-18227ce223bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21858"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e39d476-7dcb-5213-a461-df7bd1919b9f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050378}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368337}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"} :step/id #uuid "2fb61d84-6e73-5592-aa15-0582c4b7b2f2" :step/math-academy-id 607551 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222401}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222401 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222528 :tutorial/content "A forcing harmonic can cause *resonance* when its frequency matches the natural frequency of the homogeneous equation.\n\nIn cases like that, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the natural frequency.\n\nFor an equation of the form\n\n$$\ny''+\\omega^2 y = f(x),\n$$\n\nthe homogeneous solution oscillates with natural frequency $\\omega$.\n\n- **Step 2:** Find the forcing frequencies.\n\nIf $f$ is $2L$-periodic, then its Fourier series uses harmonics\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right),\\qquad \\sin\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhose frequencies are\n\n$$\n\\omega_n=\\frac{n\\pi}{L}\\qquad(n\\ge 1).\n$$\n\nA harmonic is *resonant* exactly when $\\omega_n=\\omega$.\n\n- **Step 3:** Form the particular solution ansatz for each harmonic.\n\n- For a *non-resonant* harmonic ($\\omega_n\\ne \\omega$), we use the standard ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_{p_n}=A\\cos(\\omega_n x)+B\\sin(\\omega_n x)\n$$\n\n- For a *resonant* harmonic ($\\omega_n=\\omega$), this form overlaps with the homogeneous solution. We must use the *modified ansatz* by multiplying by $x{:}$\n\n$$\ny_{p_n}=x\\left(A\\cos(\\omega x)+B\\sin(\\omega x)\\right)\n$$\n\nThis extra factor of $x$ produces a new trial function that can match the forcing term.\n\nWhen $f$ is given as a Fourier series, we check each harmonic:\n\n- *non-resonant* harmonics contribute ordinary sine/cosine terms to $y_p,$ while\n\n- *resonant* harmonics contribute terms multiplied by $x.$\n\nIn particular, the resonant harmonic is the one with index $n$ satisfying\n\n$$\n\\frac{n\\pi}{L}=\\omega.\n$$\n\nLet's see how this works in practice." :tutorial/id #uuid "1aff3ba6-cd76-5ae7-bcd6-d05e9fb44694" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14776 :tutorial/title "Cases Where the Forcing Term Induces Resonance"} :step/id #uuid "6d7e21fe-4716-5031-8d15-1ac9a8196ed5" :step/math-academy-id 607555 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222402}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222402 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221457 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221206 :question/id #uuid "926606c2-1e1a-5aab-8f2e-826163a204a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21875"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397712}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"} :step/id #uuid "ca55a627-e4f5-543f-96b1-6632f3610f35" :step/math-academy-id 607556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222403 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222530 :tutorial/content "Consider the differential equation $y''-4y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2}{n^2}\\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause $f$ has no free term and is a pure cosine series, we assume\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y''_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty -a_n n^2\\pi^2\\cos(n\\pi x)\n-\n4\\left(\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\\cos(n\\pi x)\\right)\n=\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2}{n^2}\\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty -a_n(n^2\\pi^2+4)\\cos(n\\pi x)\n=\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2}{n^2}\\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the cosine coefficients, we obtain\n\n$$\n-a_n(n^2\\pi^2+4)=\\dfrac{2}{n^2}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\na_n=-\\dfrac{2}{n^2(n^2\\pi^2+4)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a Fourier series for a particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\: -\\dfrac{2}{n^2(n^2\\pi^2+4)} \\cdot \\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f2d6d8f9-00d1-552b-889a-e54ed8d22d21" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14873 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example of Solving a Second Order ODE"} :step/id #uuid "483f5b2d-f9cb-5272-a15f-759e449ff9c0" :step/math-academy-id 607559 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222400}}] :activity/title "Solving ODEs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221475}]}
